/-! # A dimension-reducing Cohen-Macaulay groupoid slice

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0461 (lemma-slice) and 04MY (situation-slice),
more-groupoids.tex (blueprint: lem-sfb-cm-groupoid-hypersurface-slice).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A locally finitely presented immersed affine slice lowers the source-fibre dimension
at the identity by one and has Cohen-Macaulay saturation at that identity.
Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0461 and 04MY
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cm-groupoid-hypersurface-slice).

The two structure maps to S and their compatibility with s and t express the relative
base. The groupoid laws imply that e, i and c are also over S. The number n is d2;
the stabilizer is the pullback along the diagonal residue-field point, not the
restriction with two independent residue-field factors. The source of the restricted
groupoid is the second projection of the iterated pullback. The equations on x and z
identify the slice identity points: an immersion is universally injective, so these
are unique. The fibre at s(e(u)) is canonically the fibre at u by the identity law.

Proof, following all nine steps of the node:
1. Apply the field-point criterion to the canonical $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(u)\to U$. Choose
   an affine open $V=\operatorname{Spec}A$ containing $u$ in which $u$ is closed, with maximal
   ideal $\mathfrak m$. Write $k=A/\mathfrak m=\kappa(u)$. Choose an affine open
   $W=\operatorname{Spec}D$ containing $e(u)$ inside $s^{-1}(V)\cap t^{-1}(V)$, and let
   $\mathfrak q$ represent $e(u)$. Both maps $s,t:A\to D$ contract $\mathfrak q$ to $\mathfrak
   m$. Their residue maps $k\to\kappa(\mathfrak q)$ are the same isomorphism: the map induced by
   $e$ is a common inverse, since $se=te=1$. In particular $e(u)$ is a $k$-rational point of
   $F_u$. Uses: lem-sfb-finite-type-field-point-characterization.
2. Put $T=\mathcal O_{R,e(u)}=D_{\mathfrak q}$ and $C=T/s(\mathfrak m)T$. The fibre-stalk
   quotient identifies $C$ with $\mathcal O_{F_u,e(u)}$. By hypothesis it is a nonzero
   Noetherian local Cohen--Macaulay ring. Its Krull dimension is $d_2$: base change of the
   locally finite type map $s$ gives a locally finite type $k$-scheme $F_u$; choose a finite-
   type affine neighbourhood of $e(u)$ in it and apply the field dimension formula. Its
   transcendence-degree term is zero by Step 1, and passing to an open neighbourhood leaves both
   the stalk and dimension at the point unchanged. Uses: lem-sbc-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient,
   lem-weil-pbc-lft-base-change, lem-kun-dimension-formula.
3. Form the restricted groupoid on $\operatorname{Spec}k$ with arrow scheme
   $H=\operatorname{Spec}k\times_{u,U,t}R\times_{s,U,u}\operatorname{Spec}k$. Its operations are
   obtained componentwise from those of $R$; the groupoid equations remain the same equations
   after pullback. Its source and target are locally of finite type. Indeed their factors are
   base changes of $s,t$ and of $u:\operatorname{Spec}k\to U$, using the cited base-change
   lemma. Their composites are locally of finite type: on affine charts a composite of two
   finitely generated algebras is generated by the union of the two finite lists of generators.
   Its stabilizer is $G_u$: on test schemes both specify an arrow with its two endpoints equal
   to the same given $k$-point. Regard these schemes as algebraic spaces by their representable
   sheaves. The two field-groupoid dimension statements, using the identity scheme charts, give
   $\dim_{e_H}H=\dim H=\dim G_u=d_1$. Uses: def-a1-groupoid-scheme, def-trf-scheme-algebraic-
   space, lem-weil-pbc-lft-base-change, lem-sfb-field-groupoid-stabilizer-dimension, lem-sfb-
   field-groupoid-constant-local-dimension.
4. The point $e_H$ is $k$-rational. The part of $H$ lying over $W$ is affine and of finite type
   over $k$: it is a closed subscheme of the affine part of the $s$-fibre, whose coordinate
   algebra is finitely generated over $k$. Apply the field dimension formula on a finite-type
   affine neighbourhood in $H$, obtaining $\dim\mathcal O_{H,e_H}=d_1$. Compute its local ring
   by taking first the $s$-fibre and then the $t$-fibre at $u$. On the affine neighbourhood $W$,
   $u\to V$ is the closed immersion defined by $\mathfrak m$. The affine pullback formula gives
   the coordinate quotient $D/(s(\mathfrak m)D+t(\mathfrak m)D)$, and localization at the
   identity gives $\mathcal O_{H,e_H}=T/(s(\mathfrak m)T+t(\mathfrak m)T)=C/J$, where $J$ is the
   ideal generated by the image of $\mathfrak m$ under $A\xrightarrow t T\to C$. Thus
   $\dim(C/J)=d_1<d_2$. These are two endpoint conditions, not the single stabilizer equation
   $s=t$ on arbitrary field-valued endpoints. Uses: lem-kun-dimension-formula, lem-weil-pbc-
   fibre-product-affine-spectra.
5. The associated primes $P_1,\ldots,P_r$ of the self-module $C$ form a finite set. For each
   $P_j$, the depth bound gives $\dim(C/P_j)\ge\operatorname{depth}C=d_2$. The ideal $J$ is not
   contained in $P_j$: otherwise $C/P_j$ is a quotient of $C/J$, and lifting prime chains along
   the quotient would give $\dim(C/P_j)\le d_1$, a contradiction. Let $Q_j$ be the inverse image
   of $P_j$ under $A\xrightarrow t T\to C$. These are prime ideals and $\mathfrak m\not\subseteq
   Q_j$. Prime avoidance supplies $f\in\mathfrak m$ outside all $Q_j$. If the associated-prime
   list were empty the zerodivisor theorem would make $0$ a nonzerodivisor on the nonzero ring
   $C$, which is impossible; thus the nonempty-list version applies. Set $a=t(f)\in T$. Its
   image $\bar a\in C$ lies in no associated prime, so the zerodivisor theorem makes
   multiplication by $\bar a$ injective. Also $a$ and $\bar a$ lie in the respective maximal
   ideals because $f\in\mathfrak m$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-associated-primes-finite,
   lem-weil-pbc-depth-le-dim-associated-prime, lem-weil-pbc-prime-avoidance, lem-weil-pbc-
   noetherian-zerodivisors-union-associated-primes.
6. Define $B=\operatorname{Spec}(A/(f))$ and $g:B\hookrightarrow V\hookrightarrow U$. The first
   map is the closed immersion defined by the principal ideal $(f)$, so $g$ is an immersion. It
   is locally of finite presentation directly from the presentation $A/(f)$ with one relation
   and no algebra generators, followed by an open immersion, which is locally of finite
   presentation. Let $b$ be the maximal ideal $\mathfrak m/(f)$. Then $g(b)=u$ and the induced
   residue map identifies $\kappa(b)=(A/(f))/(\mathfrak m/(f))=A/\mathfrak m=k$. Uses: def-weil-
   closed-immersion, def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation, lem-sbc-locally-finite-presentation-
   composition.
7. The affine pullback formula identifies the part of $B\times_{g,U,t}R$ lying over $W$ with
   $\operatorname{Spec}(D/(t(f)))$. Hence its local ring at $x=(b,e(u))$ is $T/aT$ and the map
   from $\mathcal O_{U,u}$ is induced by $s$. To prove this map flat without a Noetherian
   hypothesis on $U$, apply the existing fibre-regular slicing lemma to $s:R\to U$ at $e(u)$ and
   the one-element germ $a=t(f)$. Step 5 makes this singleton a regular sequence in its fibre
   ring $C$ (it is a nonzerodivisor in the maximal ideal). The lemma supplies a local cut, flat
   over $U$, by a section with this very germ. Its stalk map is exactly $\mathcal O_{U,u}\to
   T/aT$. Thus $h$ is flat at $x$, irrespective of whether the neighbourhood supplied by that
   lemma equals our original $W$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra, lem-sfb-
   scheme-fibre-regular-slice-flat.
8. The fibre-stalk quotient applied to $h$ gives $\mathcal O_{h^{-1}(u),x}=(T/aT)/s(\mathfrak
   m)(T/aT)=C/(\bar a)$. The Cohen--Macaulay nonzerodivisor quotient theorem says this is
   Noetherian local of dimension $d_2-1$ and depth at least $d_2-1$. Its depth is also at most
   its dimension by the depth--support bound applied to its nonzero self-module (whose support
   is the whole prime spectrum). Thus depth equals dimension. Together with Step 7 this proves
   that $h$ is Cohen--Macaulay at $x$. Uses: lem-sbc-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient, lem-weil-pbc-
   cm-local-quotient-nonzerodivisor-cm, lem-weil-pbc-depth-le-support-dimension.
9. Finally $s_B^{-1}(b)\cong h^{-1}(u)\times_{\operatorname{Spec}k}\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(b)$
   by associating the fibre products in $R_B=B\times_{g,U,t}R\times_{s,U,g}B$. Step 6 makes the
   latter field base change the identity, and sends $e_B(b)$ to $x$. Near this point the fibre
   has affine equation $t(f)=0$ in the finite-type $k$-scheme $F_u$, so it has a finite-type
   affine neighbourhood. The field dimension formula has zero transcendence-degree term because
   the point has residue field $k$. Its topological dimension at the point therefore equals the
   local-ring dimension from Step 8, namely $d_2-1$. This is precisely the remaining assertion.
   Uses: def-pc-fibre-product, lem-kun-dimension-formula.
-/
theorem exists_cm_groupoid_hypersurface_slice
    {S U R : Scheme.{u}} (p : U ⟶ S) (q : R ⟶ S)
    (s t : R ⟶ U) (e : U ⟶ R) (i : R ⟶ R) (c : pullback s t ⟶ R)
    (hG : IsGroupoidScheme s t e i c)
    (hs : s ≫ p = q) (ht : t ≫ p = q)
    [Flat s] [Flat t] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation s] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation t]
    (hcm : ∀ (f : R ⟶ U), f = s ∨ f = t → ∀ r : R,
      IsNoetherianRing ((f.fiber (f r)).presheaf.stalk (f.asFiber r)) ∧
      (IsLocalRing.depth (R := (f.fiber (f r)).presheaf.stalk (f.asFiber r))
        ((f.fiber (f r)).presheaf.stalk (f.asFiber r)) : WithBot ℕ∞) =
          ringKrullDim ((f.fiber (f r)).presheaf.stalk (f.asFiber r)))
    (u : U) [LocallyOfFiniteType (U.fromSpecResidueField u)]
    [QuasiCompact (U.fromSpecResidueField u)] (n : ℕ)
    (hdim : topologicalKrullDimAt (s.asFiber (e u)) = (n : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hdrop : topologicalKrullDim
      (pullback (pullback.lift s t (hs.trans ht.symm))
        (pullback.lift (U.fromSpecResidueField u) (U.fromSpecResidueField u) rfl) :
          Scheme.{u}) <
          (n : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    ∃ (B : Scheme.{u}) (g : B ⟶ U) (b : B),
      IsAffine B ∧ IsImmersion g ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation g ∧
      g b = u ∧ IsIso (g.residueFieldMap b) ∧
      ∃ x : (pullback g t : Scheme.{u}),
        pullback.fst g t x = b ∧ pullback.snd g t x = e u ∧
        ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).stalkMap x).hom.Flat ∧
        IsNoetherianRing
          (((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).fiber ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s) x)).presheaf.stalk
            ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).asFiber x)) ∧
        (IsLocalRing.depth
          (R := ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).fiber ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s) x)).presheaf.stalk
            ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).asFiber x))
          (((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).fiber ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s) x)).presheaf.stalk
            ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).asFiber x)) : WithBot ℕ∞) =
          ringKrullDim
            (((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).fiber ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s) x)).presheaf.stalk
              ((pullback.snd g t ≫ s).asFiber x)) ∧
        ∃ z : (pullback.snd (pullback.snd g t ≫ s) g).fiber b,
          pullback.fst (pullback.snd g t ≫ s) g
            ((pullback.snd (pullback.snd g t ≫ s) g).fiberι b z) = x ∧
          topologicalKrullDimAt z = ((n - 1 : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
